Pass the relative fields when reporting relative motion

The SDL2 backend decides an event is relative and then hands
SDL_SendMouseMotion the absolute coordinates anyway, so every motion moves
the pointer by its own position on screen instead of by how far it moved.

The effect is a pointer that only ever travels right and down, in jumps of
hundreds of pixels, until it reaches the bottom-right corner and stays
there. Measured on an ArkOS R36S driven by gptokeyb2: 394 events carrying a
negative component, and not one negative relative value reaching the
application.

Which devices hit it depends on `e.motion.which`, the SDL2 mouse instance
id, being non-zero -- gptokeyb2's virtual pointer is, a plain one often is
not -- so the same build behaves correctly on one handheld and not on the
next.

--- a/src/video/sdl2/SDL_sdl2events.c
+++ b/src/video/sdl2/SDL_sdl2events.c
@@ -113,10 +113,15 @@
         case SDL2_MOUSEMOTION: {
             SDL_Window *win = FindWindowBySDL2ID(e.motion.windowID);
             if (win) {
                 bool relative = (e.motion.which != 0); /* rough check */
                 SDL_SendMouseMotion(timestamp, win, SDL_GLOBAL_MOUSE_ID,
                                     relative,
-                                    (float)e.motion.x, (float)e.motion.y);
+                                    /* Relative motion is xrel/yrel; x/y is
+                                       where the pointer now is, which as a
+                                       delta is both far too large and never
+                                       negative. */
+                                    relative ? (float)e.motion.xrel : (float)e.motion.x,
+                                    relative ? (float)e.motion.yrel : (float)e.motion.y);
             }
             break;
         }
